# sent_id = job42.11.1
# text = Αὐτόματοι δ' οἱ πάντες ὁμιλαδὸν ἦλθον ἀδελφεοί, Πᾶσαί τ' αὐτοκασίγνηται, πηοί τε, ἔται τε, Οἳ δ' ἄρ ὃι κεδνότατοι τὸ πρὶν καὶ φίλτατοι ἦσαν, Αἰεὶ δ' ἐν δαίτῃσι καὶ εἰλαπίνῃσι παρέσταν Αὐτοῦ ἐνὶ μεγάροισι, παραφασίῃσι δ' ἔθελγον Οὐ νόον, αἱμυλίοις τε λόγοις ἄχος ἐξακέσαντο, Καὶ παραμυθήσαντο, καὶ ἔστενον οἷα πέπονθεν, Οἷά τ' οϊζύσασκε Θεοῦ ἰότητι μογήσας, (Ἔτλη δ' ὃι ἔπω τις ἐπιχθόνιος βροτὸς ἄλλος) Ἀργύρειον δ' αὐτῷ καὶ χρύσεον ὅρμον ἔδωκαν Ἀνθρακὰς, ἀγλαὰ δῶρα, κακῆς παραμύθιον ἄτης.
# source_parser = pyaegean grc-joint-v3
# reading_translation = All his brothers came of their own accord in a throng, and all his own sisters, and kinsmen by marriage, and clansmen; and those who had been his most honored and dearest before stood by him always at feasts and banquets in his own halls, and with consolations they soothed his mind, and with winning words they healed his grief, and comforted him, and lamented what he had suffered, and how he had toiled in misery by the will of God — (such a thing some other mortal on earth endured) — and they gave him a necklace of silver and gold, glowing gifts, a comfort for his evil ruin.
# literal_translation = Self-moved but the all in-a-throng came brothers, All and own-sisters, kin-by-marriage and, clansmen and, Who but then him most-honored the before and dearest were, Always and in feasts and banquets stood-by His in halls, with-consolations but they-soothed Not mind, winning and words grief they-healed, And they-comforted, and they-lamented such-as he-has-suffered, Such-as and he-was-wont-to-mourn of-God by-will having-toiled, (He-endured but him as-some-one earthly mortal other) Silver and him and golden necklace they-gave Glowing, splendid gifts, of-evil comfort of-ruin.
